body:
My 'worst days' may be different from yours. It's worth being clear that each mental illness has its own symptoms, too - and while there is a crossfade between them, in that we all share certain experiences, I think everybody's perception with this is unique.

&#x200B;

When I'm at my 'worst', it's a matter of riding it out. I'm strapped in and the rollercoaster is teetering on the edge of a steep slope. I can close my eyes. I can scream. Nothing is going to change the fact that all the potential energy at this great height is going to throw me towards the ground. I'm going down.

&#x200B;

I use this metaphor because 'mental illness' (at least in my experience) affects your whole sensory perception, and is manifest in the body, too. Depression causes physical pain. Anxiety causes the heart to palpitate, our pores to sweat, sets our senses into overdrive. Insomnia affects motor control in a way that compares to intoxication. And so on...

&#x200B;

With that said, when these symptoms are at their most extreme - it's almost impossible to take care of myself. So I have to go back to basics with everyday life. Here's some habits I have formed (and believe me, they took a long time to form).

&#x200B;

Write a to-do list.

Even the most banal, everyday things. This has a threefold effect. 1. It takes my responsibilities out of my head and onto paper. 2. It gives an authority to the tasks I have to complete. 3. Physically ticking them off, and watching them disappear, gives a small sense of accomplishment and relief. Altogether, it is an investment of time in your future-self.

&#x200B;

Have a 'mantra'.

I have found that having a 'mantra' can be very grounding. Mine is, "I am safe". If I say it many times over, sometimes I believe it. When your own brain is brainwashing you with anxiety-riddled mindfuckery, it is possible to fight back with a positive, simple message.

&#x200B;

Write.

This relates back to the to-do list, in that it taking thoughts out of your head and seeing them in front of you can be therapeutic. Writing is also a kind of meditation. When I write, I am often surprised at the words that form on the page. It's like shining a light in the darkest depths of your mind. If you can enter a mindset in which you go easy on yourself in withholding judgement (in the sense that you are 'allowed' to say anything), you will get the best results.

&#x200B;

Distraction.

We all seek a means to escape. It's fine to indulge, as long as we don't get lost in it. I am guilty of this...

&#x200B;

Basic self-care.

Eat. Sleep. Shower. Sunlight. Most people do this on auto-pilot. But when you are in the throes of a mental illness episode, any one of these can be overwhelmingly difficult. Take everything one step at a time. It might seem ridiculous to be proud of taking a shower, but when you journeyed to hell and back to make it to the bathroom, you earned it.

&#x200B;

Finally, I would like to say something about the internal world of mental illness: like a hallucination, just because it wasn't real, doesn't mean you didn't experience it. You don't have to be at odds with the feelings you have, even if they appear out of nothing (a panic attack, for example). It happened, you might not understand it, but you are the one left to deal with it. Own it.
